---
interface Props {
  slug: string
}

const { slug } = Astro.props
---

{slug === "bertrand-paradox-economics" && (
  <figure class="flex flex-col gap-3 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
    <svg
      viewBox="0 0 420 200"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="bertrand-fig-title bertrand-fig-desc"
      class="w-full"
    >
      <title id="bertrand-fig-title">Two shops undercutting one another</title>
      <desc id="bertrand-fig-desc">
        Shop A and Shop B start at 12 pounds with a unit cost of 8 pounds. One cuts to 11, the
        other to 10, and competition continues until both charge 8 pounds. Prices are
        illustrative, not an exact model of every price step.
      </desc>
      <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
        <rect x="10" y="12" width="190" height="136" />
        <rect x="220" y="12" width="190" height="136" />
        <path d="M10 168 H410" stroke-dasharray="7 6" />
      </g>
      <g fill="currentColor" font-size="18" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">
        <text x="105" y="44">
          Shop A
        </text>
        <text x="315" y="44">
          Shop B
        </text>
        <text x="105" y="88">
          £12 → £11 → £9
        </text>
        <text x="315" y="88">
          £12 → £10 → £8
        </text>
        <text x="105" y="128" font-weight="bold">
          Final: £8
        </text>
        <text x="315" y="128" font-weight="bold">
          Final: £8
        </text>
        <text x="210" y="192">
          Unit cost: £8
        </text>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">
      Illustrative price cuts. In the model, any price above cost can be undercut. At £8, a
      further cut would make a loss.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
)}
{slug === "rietdijk-putnam-argument" && (
  <figure class="flex flex-col gap-3 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
    <svg
      viewBox="0 0 420 290"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="rp-fig-title rp-fig-desc"
      class="w-full"
    >
      <title id="rp-fig-title">Different lines of simultaneity</title>
      <desc id="rp-fig-desc">
        Two observers meet at the origin. The stationary observer's simultaneous events lie on a
        horizontal line. A relatively moving observer's simultaneous events lie on a tilted line
        through the same meeting point. A distant event can be future to one observer but
        simultaneous for the other.
      </desc>
      <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
        <path d="M30 255 H400 M90 270 V20" />
        <path d="M90 150 H390" stroke="#387a91" stroke-width="4" />
        <path d="M90 150 L390 95" stroke="#bd4b37" stroke-width="4" />
        <path d="M90 150 L165 55" stroke-dasharray="8 6" />
      </g>
      <circle cx="330" cy="106" r="7" fill="#bd4b37" />
      <circle cx="90" cy="150" r="7" fill="currentColor" />
      <g fill="currentColor" font-size="18" font-family="sans-serif">
        <text x="100" y="26">
          Time
        </text>
        <text x="400" y="280" text-anchor="end">
          Space
        </text>
        <text x="390" y="178" text-anchor="end">
          Observer A: now
        </text>
        <text x="390" y="76" text-anchor="end">
          Observer B: now
        </text>
        <text x="330" y="136" text-anchor="middle">
          Event
        </text>
        <text x="100" y="176">
          Meet
        </text>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">
      Schematic spacetime diagram, not to scale. The lines show different answers to which
      distant events happen now. Whether that makes the future already real is a separate claim.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
)}
{slug === "lighthouse-paradox" && (
  <figure class="flex flex-col gap-3 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
    <svg
      viewBox="0 0 420 260"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="light-fig-title light-fig-desc"
      class="w-full"
    >
      <title id="light-fig-title">A sweeping lighthouse beam</title>
      <desc id="light-fig-desc">
        Two light rays leave a lighthouse at slightly different angles and meet far-apart points
        on a distant wall. The spots can change position along the wall faster than light even
        though the individual rays travel outwards at light speed.
      </desc>
      <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
        <path d="M370 30 V215" stroke-width="6" />
        <path d="M50 200 L370 70" stroke="#bd4b37" stroke-width="4" />
        <path d="M50 200 L370 170" stroke="#387a91" stroke-width="4" />
        <path d="M370 70 V170" stroke-dasharray="6 6" stroke-width="4" />
      </g>
      <g fill="#d7a13d" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
        <path d="M22 216 H78 L70 232 H30 Z" />
        <rect x="40" y="182" width="20" height="34" />
        <circle cx="50" cy="200" r="6" />
      </g>
      <g fill="currentColor" font-size="18" font-family="sans-serif">
        <text x="50" y="254" text-anchor="middle">
          Lighthouse
        </text>
        <text x="360" y="40" text-anchor="end">
          Wall
        </text>
        <text x="380" y="78">
          A
        </text>
        <text x="380" y="178">
          B
        </text>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">
      The wall receives separate rays, not a sideways-moving object. A moving patch of light is
      not a message travelling from point A to point B along the wall.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
)}
